The mirrin programs built from it also contain the Go standard library and
third-party modules under their own licences. THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES lists every
one with its licence text. Among them:

- The WhatsApp channel links go.mau.fi/whatsmeow and go.mau.fi/util
  (MPL-2.0) and go.mau.fi/libsignal (GPL-3.0), so the default programs, which
  include it, are distributed as a whole under GPL-3.0.
- A program built with -tags nowhatsapp leaves those out, and is distributed
  under MIT.
- Both builds of mirrin, and mirrin-relay, contain github.com/hashicorp/yamux
  (MPL-2.0). Its own files stay under the MPL, a file-level copyleft, and
  their source is in every release's source archive.

docs/licensing.md explains what that means for the programs published with
each release.

Every mirrin program carries LICENSE and THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES with it:
`mirrin licenses` prints them, Mirrin.app keeps them in Contents/Resources,
and the Windows setup program installs them beside mirrin.exe.
